1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,683
YA-NEC course, lesson 3: the EWE receiving antenna by Floyd Koontz WA2WVL (QST, February 1995), for 160 m. Built from scratch over real ground, then RDF and F/B.

2
00:00:11,733 --> 00:00:14,747
New: an empty model.

3
00:00:14,797 --> 00:00:24,871
Frequency 1.830 MHz, span 100 kHz.

4
00:00:24,921 --> 00:00:34,138
First an important setting: an EWE has its wires connected to the ground, so in Settings we choose Sommerfeld ground (GN 2).

5
00:00:34,188 --> 00:00:43,471
The fast reflection-coefficient ground (GN 0) is fine for antennas well above ground, not for wires that go down to the soil.

6
00:00:43,521 --> 00:00:50,804
Three wires, like an upside-down U. Wire 1: a vertical 3.05 m (10 ft) tall, from the ground up.

7
00:00:50,854 --> 00:00:56,948
Wire 2: the horizontal top wire, 9.1 m (30 ft) long, at 3.05 m.

8
00:00:56,998 --> 00:01:03,452
Wire 3: the second vertical, back down to the ground. Radius 1 mm.

9
00:01:03,502 --> 00:01:07,520
Apply and recalculate.

10
00:01:07,570 --> 00:01:15,022
The feed point is at the base of the first vertical: w1b, wire 1, beginning.

11
00:01:15,072 --> 00:01:23,558
The termination resistor goes at the base of the other vertical: in Model objects, Loads, type w3e (wire 3, end).

12
00:01:23,608 --> 00:01:35,008
Type R+jX and 850 ohm, then Apply and recalculate.

13
00:01:35,058 --> 00:01:41,742
In free space the numbers are meaningless: an EWE works only together with the ground.

14
00:01:41,792 --> 00:01:50,082
So: Ground, Average (er 13, sigma 0.005 S/m). The wires stay connected to the soil.

15
00:01:50,132 --> 00:01:57,949
Now it is an EWE: 812 - j529 ohm, F/B 30.8 dB, maximum at azimuth 180 degrees, 32 degrees of elevation.

16
00:01:57,999 --> 00:02:05,482
Gain -28 dBi: very low, normal for a receiving antenna. What matters is how much noise it rejects.

17
00:02:05,532 --> 00:02:09,548
Patterns.

18
00:02:09,598 --> 00:02:20,215
A cardioid in azimuth: the lobe points toward the feed end (Koontz: 'the end with the balun and the coax'), with a deep null toward the resistor.

19
00:02:20,265 --> 00:02:32,125
The termination sets the null. Let us try 400 ohm instead of 850.

20
00:02:32,175 --> 00:02:46,642
F/B drops from 30.8 to 13.7 dB: the resistor is the 'tuning knob' of an EWE. Back to 850 ohm.

21
00:02:46,692 --> 00:02:52,909
The Receiving antennas tab has a ready EWE calculator with the same dimensions.

22
00:02:52,959 --> 00:02:59,109
It also adds 20 ohm for the ground rods, which in real life are never perfect.

23
00:02:59,159 --> 00:03:10,747
Create the NEC model and compute the RDF.

24
00:03:10,797 --> 00:03:20,681
RDF 7.7 dB: gain in the wanted direction minus the average gain in all directions (W8JI). Plus 2.7 dB of S/N compared with a vertical.

25
00:03:20,731 --> 00:03:30,147
In orange its place in the RDF ranking of the classic receiving antennas, between Flag and Pennant. Transformer 16:1 on 75 ohm.

26
00:03:30,197 --> 00:03:56,215
'Optimize for F/B' tries terminations from 200 to 1500 ohm.

27
00:03:56,265 --> 00:04:06,282
Best F/B with 800 ohm: F/B 34.2 dB, RDF 7.8 dB. In the field you trim the real resistor (a potentiometer) on a known signal from behind.

28
00:04:06,332 --> 00:04:13,282
A small, cheap antenna with a real gain in S/N on the low bands. YA-NEC is free: github.com/ic8tem/YA-NEC - 73 de IC8TEM
